{"id":"neet-2026r-q005","pn":5,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"EM wave in a dielectric medium","q":"An electromagnetic wave travelling in a lossless dielectric medium having a dielectric constant, εᵣ = 9, has the electric field, Eₓ = E₀ sin (kz – 2π × 10⁶t) Vm⁻¹ where E₀ is the amplitude and k is the wave vector. Among the following options, the incorrect choice is","o":["The wavelength of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 300 m","The magnetic field is given by the relation B_y = (B₀/v) sin(kz – 2π × 10⁶t) where v is the speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium","The direction of propagation of the electromagnetic wave is along +z","The speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 10⁸ ms⁻¹"],"c":0,"s":"Eₓ = E₀ sin(kz – 2π × 10⁶t) = E₀ sin(kz – ωt).\nSo the wave travels along +z.\nSpeed in the medium: vₘ = v₀/√εᵣ = c/√9 = (3 × 10⁸)/3 = 10⁸ m/s.\nFrequency f₀ = ω/2π = 10⁶ Hz, and it stays constant.\nWavelength in the medium: λₘ = vₘ/f₀ = 10⁸/(2π × 10⁶/2π) = 100 m.\nSo the wavelength inside the medium is 100 m, not 300 m, and option (1) is incorrect.\nNote: in option (2), B₀ should be replaced by E₀.\nNote: Option (2) as printed uses B₀ where E₀ is meant (publisher notes this); answer (1) is still the incorrect statement"},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q018","pn":18,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"EM spectrum applications","q":"The following table presents the part of the electromagnetic spectrum and their corresponding major applications.\nP. Microwave — I. For purifying the water\nQ. UV rays — II. For warming the food\nR. Gamma rays — III. For AM and FM communication systems\nS. Radio wave — IV. For treating the Cancer cells\nThe correct option is:","o":["P-I, Q-IV, R-II, S-III","P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III","P-II, Q-IV, R-III, S-I","P-I, Q-II, R-III, S-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Microwave is used for warming food.\nUV rays are used for purifying water.\nGamma rays are used for treating cancer cells.\nRadio waves are used for AM and FM communication systems.\nSo P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q040","pn":40,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Production of EM waves","q":"Match List I with List II:\nList-I (Electromagnetic wave) — List-II (Production)\nA. Microwave — I. Electrons in atoms emit light when they move from a higher energy level to a lower energy level\nB. Visible light — II. Radioactive decay of nucleus\nC. Gamma rays — III. Vibration of atoms and molecules\nD. Infra-red rays — IV. Klystron valve or magnetron valve\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"c":3,"s":"Microwaves are produced by a klystron valve or magnetron valve: A-IV.\nVisible light comes from electrons in atoms moving from a higher to a lower energy level: B-I.\nGamma rays come from radioactive decay of the nucleus: C-II.\nInfra-red rays come from vibration of atoms and molecules: D-III."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q009","pn":9,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Relation between E and B in EM wave","q":"The electric field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by\nE_z = 60 cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) V/m.\nThen expression for the corresponding magnetic field is (here subscripts denote the direction of the field) :","o":["B_y = 2 × 10⁻⁷ cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T","B_x = 2 × 10⁻⁷ cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T","B_z = 60cos (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T","B_y = 60 sin (5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t)T"],"c":0,"s":"In an electromagnetic wave, E and B are in the same phase and B₀ = E₀/c; their directions are perpendicular to each other.\n∴ B_y = (60/c) cos(5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) T\n= (60/(3 × 10⁸)) cos(5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) T\nB_y = 2 × 10⁻⁷ cos(5x + 1.5 × 10⁹ t) T"},
{"id":"neet-2025-q026","pn":26,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Displacement current","q":"A parallel plate capacitor made of circular plates is being charged such that the surface charge density on its plates is increasing at a constant rate with time. The magnetic field arising due to displacement current is:","o":["Zero at all places","Constant between the plates and zero outside the plates","Non-zero everywhere with maximum at the imaginary cylindrical surface connecting peripheries of the plates","Zero between the plates and non-zero outside"],"c":2,"s":"Let the surface charge density be σ = q/A.\nGiven dσ/dt = constant.\nSo d/dt (q/A) = constant, which gives i/A = constant (i is the current, I/A in the print).\nIt means the displacement current is constant.\nThis system acts like a cylindrical wire.\nThe graph of magnetic field B vs r is shown: B rises linearly with r inside, is maximum at the edge, then falls outside.","sfig":{"w":200,"h":155,"alt":"Graph of B against r: straight rise from the origin to a peak, then a falling curve that flattens towards the r-axis","d":[["l",20,137,20,7,"a"],["l",20,137,185,137,"a"],["t",30,18,"B"],["t",190,145,"r"],["p","M20 137 L100 60 C115 95 140 120 177 123","B"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q037","pn":37,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Displacement current","q":"A parallel plate capacitor is charged by connecting it to a battery through a resistor. If I is the current in the circuit, then in the gap between the plates;","o":["displacement current of magnitude equal to I flows in a direction opposite to that of I.","displacement current of magnitude greater than I flows but can be in any direction.","there is no current.","displacement current of magnitude equal to I flows in the same direction as I."],"c":3,"s":"Use the modified Ampere's law: ∮B·dℓ = μ₀(I_C + I_D).\nLoop L1 is around the wire: I_C ≠ 0 and I_D = 0.\nLoop L2 is in the gap between the plates: I_C = 0 and I_D ≠ 0.\nBy current continuity (like KCL), I_C = I_D.\nSo the displacement current equals I and has the same direction.","sfig":{"w":230,"h":100,"alt":"A capacitor with two vertical plates; a wire with a rightward arrow reaches the left plate and a wire leaves the right plate. Dashed loop L1 encircles the wire left of the left plate and dashed loop L2 lies in the gap between the plates.","d":[["l",3,50,38,50],["l",38,50,48,50,"a"],["l",48,50,57,50],["l",57,17,57,83],["l",143,17,143,83],["l",143,50,223,50],["p","M43 17 A14 33 0 1 0 43 83 A14 33 0 1 0 43 17 Z","d"],["p","M112 17 A15 33 0 1 0 112 83 A15 33 0 1 0 112 17 Z","d"],["t",36,11,"L₁"],["t",104,11,"L₂"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q038","pn":38,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Properties of EM waves","q":"The property which is not of an electromagnetic wave travelling in free space is that;","o":["they travel with a speed equal to 1/√(μ₀ε₀)","they originate from charges moving with uniform speed.","they are transverse in nature.","they energy density in electric field is equal to energy density in magnetic field."],"c":1,"s":"EM waves come from an accelerating charge.\nA charge moving with uniform velocity gives only a steady magnetic field, not a wave."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q031","pn":31,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Relation between E0 and B0","q":"In a plane electromagnetic wave travelling in free space, the electric field component oscillates sinusoidally at a frequency of 2.0 × 10¹⁰ Hz and amplitude 48 V m⁻¹. Then the amplitude of oscillating magnetic field is : (Speed of light in free space = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)","o":["1.6 × 10⁻⁶ T","1.6 × 10⁻⁹ T","1.6 × 10⁻⁸ T","1.6 × 10⁻⁷ T"],"c":3,"s":"For an electromagnetic wave, c = E₀/B₀.\nE₀ is the electric field amplitude and B₀ is the magnetic field amplitude.\nB₀ = 48/(3×10⁸) = 1.6×10⁻⁷ T."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q013","pn":13,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Speed of light in a medium","q":"When light propagates through a material medium of relative permittivity εr and relative permeability μr, the velocity of light, v is given by (c-velocity of light in vacuum)","o":["v = √(μr/εr)","v = √(εr/μr)","v = c/√(εrμr)","v = c"],"c":2,"s":"In a medium v = 1/√(εμ) = 1/√(ε₀εr μ₀μr).\nIn vacuum c = 1/√(ε₀μ₀).\nSo v = c/√(εr μr)."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q035","pn":35,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Electromagnetic Waves","st":"Wavelengths of EM spectrum","q":"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Electromagnetic waves): (a) AM radio waves (b) Microwaves (c) Infrared radiations (d) X-rays. List-II (Wavelength): (i) 10⁻¹⁰ m (ii) 10² m (iii) 10⁻² m (iv) 10⁻⁴ m. Choose the correct answer from the options given below","o":["(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)"],"c":2,"s":"AM radio waves have wavelength about 10² m.\nMicrowaves have about 10⁻² m.\nInfrared radiations have about 10⁻⁴ m.\nX-rays have about 10⁻¹⁰ m.\nSo (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)."}
